WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.276
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.464 --> 00:00:07.880
WILLEM PIENAAR:
My name is Willem.

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:11.220
I'm the data science
platform lead for Go-Jek.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:13.610
Today we're going to be talking
about some of the lessons

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:16.790
we learned scaling machine
learning at Go-Jek on Google

00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:18.779
Cloud.

00:00:18.779 --> 00:00:20.820
So if we're all ready,
I'm going to just dive in.

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:25.175
So who or what is Go-Jek?

00:00:25.175 --> 00:00:29.070
So, Go-Jek is an Indonesian
technology startup.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:31.510
So we have a wide variety
of products and services,

00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:34.190
and some of them are on
the screen right now.

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:36.050
The one that we're
most famous for

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:39.500
is our ride-hailing
service, Go-Ride.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:43.790
And the photo actually shows
you what the service does.

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:46.790
It's a woman being taken
on one of these motorcycles

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:48.650
to a destination.

00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:51.740
The reason why it's not a
car and it's a motorcycle

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:54.432
is because of the unique
challenges in Indonesia.

00:00:54.432 --> 00:00:55.890
So a lot of people
don't know this,

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:59.450
but Indonesia is one of
the largest countries

00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:00.800
in the world by population.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:03.000
They have over 260
million people.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.580
So it's the fourth
largest country

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:07.230
in the world by population.

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:09.290
And they have a lot
of unique challenges

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:12.860
with public infrastructure
and traffic congestion.

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:15.110
And the way that Indonesians
have solved this problem

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:17.360
is by using motorcycles
to get around.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:19.460
And they also have their
own motorcycle taxis

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:20.900
called "ojeks."

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:25.190
And when we originally started,
we started as a call center

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:26.180
for ojeks.

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:28.760
So it was offline,
there's no application,

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:30.720
it's completely disconnected.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:32.540
You just call in
and we sent an ojek

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:35.640
to take you to your destination.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:39.230
So originally our founders
launched the service.

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:42.560
And there was great
growth and demand for it

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:45.290
because the traffic
is so bad in Indonesia

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:47.450
that the service
really solved work day

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:49.190
problems for Indonesians.

00:01:49.190 --> 00:01:51.980
And they continued to collect
information about what products

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:54.590
our customers
wanted in Indonesia.

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:58.835
And in 2015, we launched our
first mobile application.

00:01:58.835 --> 00:02:01.730
It was just a modest
bundle of products.

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:04.500
So, the key products there
are Go-Ride, the ride-hailing

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:07.010
on motorcycles;
Go-Food, so that's

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:09.289
food delivery also
in motorcycles;

00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:12.380
and then things like Go-Mart--
so it's grocery shopping--

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:14.480
and other logistic services.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:17.720
And when we launched
this initial application,

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:20.250
the uptake was incredible.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:23.340
So we hit hyper
growth very quickly.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:25.610
And the demand in
Indonesia was insatiable

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:28.130
because it solved so many
of the daily problems

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:32.200
that Indonesians had
getting around the city.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:34.250
And so over the
next couple of years

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:37.310
we launched a bunch of
products, not just at random--

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:39.210
we've always been a very
date-driven company.

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:43.640
We've always looked at what the
customers wanted and launched

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:45.980
very targeted products to them.

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:48.380
So currently we
are a unicorn, one

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:50.240
of the few unicorns in Asia--

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:52.400
or in Southeast Asia.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:54.080
And we've got 18
products that we've

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:56.330
launched in the application
and many more that

00:02:56.330 --> 00:02:58.070
are not in the application.

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:03.920
In 2018 we are focusing on
international expansion.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:06.830
But let's talk a bit
about our home, Indonesia.

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:10.050
So, our app has been downloaded
almost 80 million times.

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:13.520
We operate in 60 cities
throughout Indonesia.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:15.480
And in many of our
product verticals,

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:17.910
we're actually the
largest player.

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:20.470
So if I can just talk to
two or three of those.

00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:22.910
For ride-hailing, we have
more than a million drivers

00:03:22.910 --> 00:03:24.380
on our platform.

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:26.720
On a typical day you'll
have hundreds of thousands

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:31.650
of drivers online at the same
time servicing our customers.

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:34.430
For Go-Food, our food
delivery service,

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:37.580
we have more than 200,000
merchants on our platform.

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:40.370
And we call the merchants
because it's not just

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:41.270
restaurants.

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:45.260
It's also moms and pops
that are selling food

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:46.550
from their garages.

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:48.350
So this platform that
we've built really

00:03:48.350 --> 00:03:51.970
allows for a kind of
socioeconomic mobility.

00:03:51.970 --> 00:03:56.520
It enables people to
rise up from poverty.

00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:01.000
So the third project that I
wanted to highlight is Go-Pay.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.830
It's one of the leading
e-money platforms

00:04:03.830 --> 00:04:06.480
that we've launched
in Southeast Asia.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:07.940
So we have many
different products,

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:10.640
and I just wanted to highlight
the scale at which we're

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:11.750
operating.

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:14.630
So along with that is data.

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:18.060
So this visualization
represents some of the data

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:19.680
points that we're dealing with.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:23.690
It's a visualization of Jakarta,
the capital city of Indonesia,

00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:27.200
where each pixel represents
a person being dropped off

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:32.910
or picked up on our
Go-Ride platform.

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:34.640
So this is just one
type of data point

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:36.810
that we have on
a single product,

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:39.830
but we have many types of
products and data points.

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:43.062
And data science plays a key
role in our organization,

00:04:43.062 --> 00:04:44.270
and machine learning as well.

00:04:44.270 --> 00:04:46.262
When you have all
these decisions being

00:04:46.262 --> 00:04:47.720
made in real time,
machine learning

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:51.510
is critical to making
those decisions.

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:53.300
But the business
and the organization

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:57.350
looks to data science also
to understand our customers.

00:04:57.350 --> 00:04:59.480
And especially with
international expansion

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:02.750
looming, we wanted
to understand what

00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:04.562
the unique demands
of our customers

00:05:04.562 --> 00:05:05.645
were in different markets.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:10.100
So as a data science
platform lead,

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:12.830
it's my responsibility to make
sure our data scientists are

00:05:12.830 --> 00:05:17.346
as efficient as possible so
that their time is being used

00:05:17.346 --> 00:05:19.970
correctly and that they have the
right tools available to them.

00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:21.610
Especially with
international expansion

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:23.610
you don't want to just
add more data scientists.

00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:26.190
You want to really add
leverage to the existing ones

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:27.280
that you have.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:30.240
So I launched an investigation
into how our data scientists

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:32.220
were spending their
time to see if we

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:36.130
can't improve their efficiency.

00:05:36.130 --> 00:05:39.550
And when we launched
this investigation,

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:42.120
one of the data scientists--
on screen, Darius--

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:45.330
came to us and said, hey,
I've got a lot of projects

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:47.490
that I'm working on right now.

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:49.260
I'm actually getting
a bit swamped.

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:51.780
And maybe you can focus
your investigation on me

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:54.982
and see if you can't optimize
the way I'm doing things.

00:05:54.982 --> 00:05:56.940
Darius is very representative
of a typical data

00:05:56.940 --> 00:05:58.479
scientist in our organization.

00:05:58.479 --> 00:06:00.270
He's working on a lot
of interesting things

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:04.650
like fraud detection, driver
allocation, personalization,

00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:06.180
and forecasting.

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:08.402
So he's got projects
all across the board.

00:06:08.402 --> 00:06:10.860
And we thought that if we could
solve Darius' problems then

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:14.550
we could solve all the
data-scientist-related

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:16.020
inefficiencies.

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:18.690
So we looked at where Darius
was spending his time,

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:21.730
and this is what we found.

00:06:21.730 --> 00:06:24.930
So this is a bar of where
Darius is spending his time

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:28.050
on a typical project, so
as part of his lifecycle

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:29.190
of his project.

00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:32.640
And we honed in on the different
tasks that he was doing.

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:34.530
The gray areas
represent tasks which

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:40.134
we deem to be not really
data science per se.

00:06:40.134 --> 00:06:42.300
So these are tasks like
provisioning infrastructure,

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:43.880
installing software.

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:45.930
Its a systems and
engineering-related work

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:47.600
that Darius was doing.

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:50.160
And then if you look
at the colored blocks,

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:51.690
those are the
data-science-related

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:54.337
that he's an expert at,
that he's good at doing.

00:06:54.337 --> 00:06:55.920
So we wanted him to
focus more on that

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:57.390
and less on the engineering.

00:06:57.390 --> 00:07:01.257
And if you have a call center
on one day and then a few years

00:07:01.257 --> 00:07:02.340
later you have a unicorn--

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:04.830
billion dollar company with
thousands of employees--

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:07.620
and you tell data scientists
to deliver results,

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:09.120
then you get
something like this.

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:12.000
Because data scientists
will need to provision infra

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:14.120
if there's no platform
for them to build on.

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:16.140
They build these
end to end systems.

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:19.890
So what we told Darius
is was that we're

00:07:19.890 --> 00:07:23.020
going to try and solve your
inefficiencies and problems

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:25.320
and we're going to look
at all of your projects

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:27.660
through three different lenses.

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:29.860
The first is the
sourcing of data.

00:07:29.860 --> 00:07:31.740
The second is
feature engineering.

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:33.790
And the final one will
be machine learning.

00:07:33.790 --> 00:07:35.890
And by looking at
these three aspects,

00:07:35.890 --> 00:07:39.420
we're going to try and improve
the way you are spending

00:07:39.420 --> 00:07:42.930
your time so that you can
focus more on data science

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:45.310
and machine learning.

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:48.619
So the first of the three
is the sourcing of data.

00:07:48.619 --> 00:07:50.160
And this is a very
foundational block

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:53.400
that we had to address before we
could get onto machine learning

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:54.944
and feature engineering.

00:07:54.944 --> 00:07:57.360
So we asked Darius can you
give us an example of a project

00:07:57.360 --> 00:07:58.943
that you were working
on where you had

00:07:58.943 --> 00:08:00.750
troubles with sourcing of data.

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:02.610
And this is what he told us.

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:05.100
He says he was working on
an exploratory data analysis

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:06.060
project.

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:10.470
He had this hypothesis that if
a driver is going to pick up

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:13.440
a customer, the closer
they are to the customer,

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:15.720
the quicker it will be
to pick up that customer.

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:19.980
Except, the bit of data that
he had didn't indicate that--

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:21.640
the opposite was actually true.

00:08:21.640 --> 00:08:25.590
So, the closer the car is to
the customer, for some drivers,

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:28.000
the longer it takes them
to pick up the driver.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:30.240
And this was very
counter-intuitive.

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:32.190
So what he said
was, OK, he's going

00:08:32.190 --> 00:08:34.559
to investigate why
this is the case.

00:08:34.559 --> 00:08:38.460
So what he wanted
to do is he wanted

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:40.419
to look at the specific
drivers in more detail.

00:08:40.419 --> 00:08:43.169
So he wanted more
data on the drivers.

00:08:43.169 --> 00:08:46.740
So he asked other data
scientists in his team

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:49.170
for this data, and
they didn't have it.

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:50.460
He asked his manager.

00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:52.640
He asked the VP
of their science.

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:53.880
He asked the CTO.

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:57.450
Eventually, he was directed to
a team in Bangalore that could

00:08:57.450 --> 00:08:58.930
potentially have this data.

00:08:58.930 --> 00:09:01.180
And it turns out they
did have the data.

00:09:01.180 --> 00:09:03.990
So what they told him was that
he can't query their production

00:09:03.990 --> 00:09:06.570
database because
it'll lock the DB

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:09.850
and it will ruin the
driver experience.

00:09:09.850 --> 00:09:13.450
And so the problem here
is that this is not really

00:09:13.450 --> 00:09:15.545
Darius' job to hunt after data.

00:09:15.545 --> 00:09:16.920
So we knew that
this is something

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:20.460
that we needed to address before
we could get to any of the ML

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:22.500
or feature engineering.

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:25.380
So our realization was--

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:28.459
don't go to the data,
let the data come to you.

00:09:28.459 --> 00:09:30.500
So the key thing here is
that Darius shouldn't we

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:31.910
have to go after the data.

00:09:31.910 --> 00:09:34.890
And we realized we needed
to centralize data storage

00:09:34.890 --> 00:09:36.990
within our organization.

00:09:36.990 --> 00:09:40.380
And we did it through
the following means.

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:43.800
We said we're going to
build a data foundation.

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:46.710
And we're going to
bring in all the data

00:09:46.710 --> 00:09:49.950
from all the product teams
that are being created

00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:51.509
on a very frequent basis.

00:09:51.509 --> 00:09:53.550
And we're going to do this
with three component--

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:55.980
BigQuery, Kafka,
and Cloud Storage.

00:09:55.980 --> 00:09:58.900
So BigQuery will become
our data warehouse.

00:09:58.900 --> 00:09:59.775
This is a no-brainer.

00:09:59.775 --> 00:10:01.899
I don't know if I have to
sell anybody on BigQuery.

00:10:01.899 --> 00:10:03.450
It's big data,
storage, it scales,

00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:04.870
it's easy to access SQL.

00:10:07.546 --> 00:10:09.420
We needed some of the
functionality in Kafka,

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:11.378
and that's why we opted
for Kafka in this case.

00:10:11.378 --> 00:10:13.920
But we do use Pub/Sub as
well, but Kafka's industry

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:18.280
standard event bus, and Cloud
Storage as our data leg.

00:10:18.280 --> 00:10:21.000
The problem with this is
that even if you standardize

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:24.750
publishing of data to
a centralized location,

00:10:24.750 --> 00:10:27.930
you have to force people either
through a stick or a carrot

00:10:27.930 --> 00:10:28.950
to do that.

00:10:28.950 --> 00:10:31.080
And we wanted to actually
give them some benefits

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:32.610
or incentives to publish.

00:10:32.610 --> 00:10:34.610
So what we said
is, if you publish

00:10:34.610 --> 00:10:38.354
your data to this foundation,
we'll give you some benefits.

00:10:38.354 --> 00:10:39.770
And the benefits
that we gave them

00:10:39.770 --> 00:10:42.590
were reporting, one, so that
their managers were happy.

00:10:42.590 --> 00:10:44.840
So we automatically generate
reports based on the data

00:10:44.840 --> 00:10:46.010
that you publish there.

00:10:46.010 --> 00:10:48.260
We give you automatic archival.

00:10:48.260 --> 00:10:50.660
We give you automatic
monitoring and alerting

00:10:50.660 --> 00:10:52.640
of your events on Kafka.

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:56.330
And then finally, because you're
using BigQuery and other Google

00:10:56.330 --> 00:10:59.600
services like Cloud Storage, you
have centralized authentication

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:01.580
and authorization built in.

00:11:01.580 --> 00:11:04.280
So with all these benefits that
we gave all the product teams

00:11:04.280 --> 00:11:05.810
and other teams in
the organization,

00:11:05.810 --> 00:11:07.410
they started publishing data.

00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:10.880
And once they started publishing
data, people like Darius,

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:14.610
and analysts, and other
employees could then find data.

00:11:14.610 --> 00:11:17.190
And this led to more insights.

00:11:17.190 --> 00:11:18.950
And so what Darius
did is he went back

00:11:18.950 --> 00:11:22.430
and he looked at those
drivers, with the weird pattern

00:11:22.430 --> 00:11:25.370
of taking very long
to reach customers,

00:11:25.370 --> 00:11:27.660
and this is what he found.

00:11:27.660 --> 00:11:30.037
So here are two
images that illustrate

00:11:30.037 --> 00:11:32.120
what Darius found when he
looked at these drivers.

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:34.190
On the left there's a
driver that's permanently

00:11:34.190 --> 00:11:36.260
stationed on a building.

00:11:36.260 --> 00:11:38.510
And he's just always
sitting there.

00:11:38.510 --> 00:11:40.190
And on the right
there's a driver

00:11:40.190 --> 00:11:41.990
that is moving through
a bunch of buildings

00:11:41.990 --> 00:11:44.000
at 106 kilometers an hour.

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:45.710
Now in miles per
hour I don't know

00:11:45.710 --> 00:11:47.960
what that is-- that's like
60 or 70 miles per hour.

00:11:47.960 --> 00:11:49.850
But the point is
that it's impossible.

00:11:49.850 --> 00:11:51.770
In Jakarta nobody
moves at that speed.

00:11:51.770 --> 00:11:53.540
That's like the speed
of light in Jakarta.

00:11:53.540 --> 00:11:57.380
So what we realized is that
these drivers were actually

00:11:57.380 --> 00:11:58.850
faking their location.

00:11:58.850 --> 00:12:00.620
They were using
software that allowed

00:12:00.620 --> 00:12:02.930
them to pretend to be
at a specific location

00:12:02.930 --> 00:12:04.970
so that they could get
preferential treatment

00:12:04.970 --> 00:12:07.730
by our machine learning models
when they were being assigned

00:12:07.730 --> 00:12:08.750
to trips.

00:12:08.750 --> 00:12:10.700
So in that case, the
driver was pretending

00:12:10.700 --> 00:12:11.904
to be in the shopping mall.

00:12:11.904 --> 00:12:13.820
And then if somebody
leaves the shopping mall,

00:12:13.820 --> 00:12:15.236
he would be assigned
to that trip.

00:12:15.236 --> 00:12:17.610
But in reality, he's
actually far away

00:12:17.610 --> 00:12:19.580
And then he would
have to drive there.

00:12:19.580 --> 00:12:21.380
So, armed with this
knowledge, Darius

00:12:21.380 --> 00:12:24.350
could then go and
build a model that

00:12:24.350 --> 00:12:27.420
could identify these drivers
and then react accordingly.

00:12:27.420 --> 00:12:29.450
So the key thing
here is building

00:12:29.450 --> 00:12:32.210
a data foundation is a
foundational and fundamental

00:12:32.210 --> 00:12:34.815
part of solving ML problems.

00:12:34.815 --> 00:12:36.690
And you shouldn't be
hiring data scientists--

00:12:36.690 --> 00:12:38.090
or at least not many--

00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:41.715
until you've built
this fundamental block.

00:12:41.715 --> 00:12:43.340
So this was the first
part that we did.

00:12:45.870 --> 00:12:47.960
And feature engineering
is the second part.

00:12:47.960 --> 00:12:51.660
So we asked Darius
about what projects

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:54.390
he has in feature engineering
where he had frustrations

00:12:54.390 --> 00:12:57.510
with working with features.

00:12:57.510 --> 00:12:59.550
So he told us about
another project.

00:12:59.550 --> 00:13:03.270
And this one is a lot
more important for Go-Jek.

00:13:03.270 --> 00:13:05.670
So this is the driver
allocation problem.

00:13:05.670 --> 00:13:08.950
So Darius told us
how this works.

00:13:08.950 --> 00:13:11.040
So basically if
you're a customer

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:13.320
and you're making a
request for a booking

00:13:13.320 --> 00:13:16.650
to go to a destination, you
need to be assigned a driver.

00:13:16.650 --> 00:13:19.020
Most customers think
that, OK, they just

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:21.660
want the closest driver
that's right next to them.

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:23.780
Oftentimes you can
see on your little app

00:13:23.780 --> 00:13:25.770
there's a driver right
outside your door.

00:13:25.770 --> 00:13:30.090
And you wonder why doesn't this
algorithm allocate this driver.

00:13:30.090 --> 00:13:32.670
But it's often a
very complex process.

00:13:32.670 --> 00:13:35.357
Because sometimes drivers
want to head home.

00:13:35.357 --> 00:13:36.690
Sometimes drivers are on a trip.

00:13:36.690 --> 00:13:39.372
Sometimes they've just
made a turn onto an highway

00:13:39.372 --> 00:13:41.580
and they're going to take
a long time to turn around.

00:13:41.580 --> 00:13:43.913
Sometimes you want to optimize
for the driver experience

00:13:43.913 --> 00:13:45.690
because they're also
part of our system.

00:13:45.690 --> 00:13:47.160
So the drivers
might not have had

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:50.470
an opportunity for a long time
to take a trip with a customer.

00:13:50.470 --> 00:13:53.370
So they're also trying
to earn a living.

00:13:53.370 --> 00:13:57.240
So basically Darius realized
that the driver allocation

00:13:57.240 --> 00:14:00.824
problem is one that is
very dependent on features,

00:14:00.824 --> 00:14:03.240
because the features will
really drive the decision-making

00:14:03.240 --> 00:14:03.880
of the model.

00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:06.270
And this model is
actually extremely

00:14:06.270 --> 00:14:07.770
important for our organization.

00:14:07.770 --> 00:14:09.880
If you're doing 100 million
bookings every month,

00:14:09.880 --> 00:14:12.780
this model is going to
process a lot of money.

00:14:12.780 --> 00:14:15.330
And a small tweak to this
model can have a massive impact

00:14:15.330 --> 00:14:16.920
on the bottom line.

00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:18.900
And so Darius said,
OK, let's first

00:14:18.900 --> 00:14:20.820
make a list of all
the features we need.

00:14:20.820 --> 00:14:22.890
So he starts listing
down the features.

00:14:22.890 --> 00:14:25.770
And these are some of the
typical features that Darius--

00:14:25.770 --> 00:14:27.690
off the top of his
head-- came up with.

00:14:27.690 --> 00:14:30.000
So there are driver-related
features like what is

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:33.570
the location, speed,
direction, ETA--

00:14:33.570 --> 00:14:35.242
so, focused on the
driver features.

00:14:35.242 --> 00:14:37.200
And then there's also
customer-related features

00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:41.310
like the profile, their
clicks, or their actions,

00:14:41.310 --> 00:14:42.309
their history.

00:14:42.309 --> 00:14:44.100
Then there are special
features like what's

00:14:44.100 --> 00:14:46.170
the demand in this
area, what's the supply

00:14:46.170 --> 00:14:48.280
like-- traffic-related features.

00:14:48.280 --> 00:14:50.040
And then finally,
temporal features

00:14:50.040 --> 00:14:52.920
like what's the time of the
day, what's the day of the week,

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:54.630
is it a public holiday.

00:14:54.630 --> 00:14:57.390
Often in Indonesia you
have religious holidays

00:14:57.390 --> 00:15:00.420
that can completely change
the way that traffic behaves

00:15:00.420 --> 00:15:03.400
and how supply and
demand dynamics change.

00:15:03.400 --> 00:15:05.490
So he made this list
of features and then

00:15:05.490 --> 00:15:07.770
he set about creating
these features

00:15:07.770 --> 00:15:11.550
so that he could train
a model and then deploy

00:15:11.550 --> 00:15:13.620
his model into production.

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:15.570
But he ran into some problems.

00:15:15.570 --> 00:15:18.750
And these were some of the
problems that he ran into.

00:15:18.750 --> 00:15:22.110
So the first problem he ran into
was the volume of feature data.

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:24.210
So we had the data
foundation now.

00:15:24.210 --> 00:15:26.610
And what Darius was doing
is he was spinning up

00:15:26.610 --> 00:15:29.100
virtual machines and
he was scheduling

00:15:29.100 --> 00:15:32.220
jobs that would transform
that information into features

00:15:32.220 --> 00:15:33.570
and then publish it somewhere.

00:15:33.570 --> 00:15:35.800
And then he would train a
model on those features.

00:15:35.800 --> 00:15:36.800
This is a batch process.

00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:39.540
It's an offline process,
but it's a scheduled one.

00:15:39.540 --> 00:15:42.000
The trick here is that
for data scientists

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:44.100
that's a lot of work.

00:15:44.100 --> 00:15:45.750
These pipelines run
for hours and days.

00:15:45.750 --> 00:15:47.610
The iteration
cycle is very slow.

00:15:47.610 --> 00:15:50.350
And making a small
tweak means you have

00:15:50.350 --> 00:15:51.660
to rerun the whole pipeline.

00:15:51.660 --> 00:15:54.100
Making a mistake means you
have to start all over.

00:15:54.100 --> 00:15:57.060
So this is a frustration
that is costing us

00:15:57.060 --> 00:15:59.130
a lot of time,
data science hours,

00:15:59.130 --> 00:16:01.590
something we wanted to solve.

00:16:01.590 --> 00:16:04.170
The second problem we had
was with real-time features.

00:16:04.170 --> 00:16:07.680
So you can imagine if you
have Kafka or an EventStream,

00:16:07.680 --> 00:16:09.780
you can't just run
a query on Kafka.

00:16:09.780 --> 00:16:11.520
You need to actually
build a system that

00:16:11.520 --> 00:16:14.670
streams that data into a data
store with transformations

00:16:14.670 --> 00:16:15.990
that builds features.

00:16:15.990 --> 00:16:18.580
And then you can access
those features in real time.

00:16:18.580 --> 00:16:21.840
So every time we wanted
to launch a project,

00:16:21.840 --> 00:16:24.640
we needed an engineer to come
in and build a system that

00:16:24.640 --> 00:16:26.760
could stream these
events and build

00:16:26.760 --> 00:16:28.750
features for real-time access.

00:16:28.750 --> 00:16:31.950
So this is something we wanted
to solve across the board.

00:16:31.950 --> 00:16:34.650
The third problem we
had was consistency.

00:16:34.650 --> 00:16:37.710
We had engineers now building
features in real time,

00:16:37.710 --> 00:16:40.230
and then we had Darius
building it in batch.

00:16:40.230 --> 00:16:41.690
But these were disconnected.

00:16:41.690 --> 00:16:43.580
The real-time
features were in Java,

00:16:43.580 --> 00:16:45.840
and Go, and other
languages like Scala.

00:16:45.840 --> 00:16:48.300
And Darius was building
his features in Python.

00:16:48.300 --> 00:16:50.020
So there's an
inconsistency there.

00:16:50.020 --> 00:16:51.900
And this creates a
problem, because models

00:16:51.900 --> 00:16:53.917
are being trained on
one set of features

00:16:53.917 --> 00:16:55.500
and they're being
served in production

00:16:55.500 --> 00:16:56.600
with different features.

00:16:56.600 --> 00:16:59.760
And there's real scope
for problems to come in.

00:16:59.760 --> 00:17:01.200
Also, there's
duplication of work.

00:17:01.200 --> 00:17:05.310
You actually just want to
define a feature once, or twice.

00:17:05.310 --> 00:17:06.810
And then finally, discovery.

00:17:06.810 --> 00:17:10.200
So Darius actually went to
lunch with a data scientist

00:17:10.200 --> 00:17:12.974
and he realized that this
data scientist had already

00:17:12.974 --> 00:17:15.390
developed a lot of the features
that Darius had developed.

00:17:15.390 --> 00:17:18.647
And so Darius was
redeveloping the same features

00:17:18.647 --> 00:17:20.730
due to a lack of knowledge
and a lack of discovery

00:17:20.730 --> 00:17:21.940
in the organization.

00:17:21.940 --> 00:17:26.220
So we knew we needed to solve
this problem holistically

00:17:26.220 --> 00:17:29.520
for the whole organization so
that the discovery was there

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:31.870
and the standardization
was there.

00:17:31.870 --> 00:17:35.310
So our realization was that
features should be free.

00:17:35.310 --> 00:17:37.950
You're going to have to pay
a cost to build features

00:17:37.950 --> 00:17:39.490
the first time.

00:17:39.490 --> 00:17:41.670
But after that, it should
be available in all

00:17:41.670 --> 00:17:44.440
of your environments
consistently-- production,

00:17:44.440 --> 00:17:47.050
for serving, and for training.

00:17:47.050 --> 00:17:48.590
And it should be discoverable.

00:17:48.590 --> 00:17:51.770
And you should have information
about your features.

00:17:51.770 --> 00:17:55.010
So we were going to build
this platform for Darius

00:17:55.010 --> 00:17:58.970
to access features
and create features.

00:17:58.970 --> 00:18:02.690
And the first part of
that platform is Dataflow.

00:18:02.690 --> 00:18:05.630
So we had our data sources
now with our data foundation,

00:18:05.630 --> 00:18:08.450
and we needed a way to solve
the consistency problem.

00:18:08.450 --> 00:18:10.640
And how we did that is
by introducing Dataflow.

00:18:10.640 --> 00:18:12.250
So what's great about
Dataflow and why

00:18:12.250 --> 00:18:15.110
it's so suited for this purpose
is because we want consistency.

00:18:15.110 --> 00:18:17.780
We don't want to redefine the
same features over and over.

00:18:17.780 --> 00:18:20.510
And with Dataflow,
batch and stream

00:18:20.510 --> 00:18:22.554
are supported as
first class citizens.

00:18:22.554 --> 00:18:24.470
There's really no
distinction between the two.

00:18:24.470 --> 00:18:25.970
They're just different
data sources.

00:18:25.970 --> 00:18:29.330
So with Dataflow you can take
in CSVs, you can take in events,

00:18:29.330 --> 00:18:32.750
you can take in SQL
relational data,

00:18:32.750 --> 00:18:35.030
and then transform it with
a single transformation

00:18:35.030 --> 00:18:37.400
and produce it into
any kind of data store.

00:18:37.400 --> 00:18:41.370
So by introducing Dataflow,
we have a single place where

00:18:41.370 --> 00:18:44.564
Darius can define his feature.

00:18:44.564 --> 00:18:46.230
Some of the other
advantages of Dataflow

00:18:46.230 --> 00:18:48.960
is that it automatically scales.

00:18:48.960 --> 00:18:51.235
There's no servers to manage.

00:18:51.235 --> 00:18:53.360
There's no lock-in, because
the code that you write

00:18:53.360 --> 00:18:56.390
on Dataflow, the
API is called Beam--

00:18:56.390 --> 00:18:57.720
Apache Beam.

00:18:57.720 --> 00:18:59.180
It can be run on Flink.

00:18:59.180 --> 00:19:00.950
It can be run on Spark.

00:19:00.950 --> 00:19:03.710
So there's a portability to the
code you write so you're not

00:19:03.710 --> 00:19:06.050
locked into Dataflow.

00:19:06.050 --> 00:19:07.790
So some of the
challenges with Dataflow

00:19:07.790 --> 00:19:11.540
is that because it's streaming
in batch in all cases,

00:19:11.540 --> 00:19:13.350
the API is tricky.

00:19:13.350 --> 00:19:16.430
You're also limited by the fact
that Python and Go lang support

00:19:16.430 --> 00:19:18.260
is not as featureful as Java.

00:19:18.260 --> 00:19:21.810
So in most cases, you'll be
forced to write Java code.

00:19:21.810 --> 00:19:24.925
So that's the trick
with Beam and Dataflow.

00:19:24.925 --> 00:19:26.524
So often the case
with this is you'll

00:19:26.524 --> 00:19:28.940
need an engineer to help define
the features with the data

00:19:28.940 --> 00:19:29.440
scientist.

00:19:29.440 --> 00:19:32.720
But the key thing here is
once it's defined it's done--

00:19:32.720 --> 00:19:33.470
it's always there.

00:19:33.470 --> 00:19:35.270
And you can store
your features then

00:19:35.270 --> 00:19:39.740
in a training store as
well as a real-time store.

00:19:39.740 --> 00:19:42.630
So here's an example of
a feature definition.

00:19:42.630 --> 00:19:44.030
So in this case,
we're just going

00:19:44.030 --> 00:19:45.446
to look at how
many chips a driver

00:19:45.446 --> 00:19:46.870
has completed in the day.

00:19:46.870 --> 00:19:48.740
And we take trip events.

00:19:48.740 --> 00:19:50.180
This code is Python code.

00:19:50.180 --> 00:19:55.135
And then you apply P
transforms on the P collection.

00:19:55.135 --> 00:19:56.510
So the trip event
is a collection

00:19:56.510 --> 00:19:58.940
of events or elements.

00:19:58.940 --> 00:20:01.100
And then you apply
subsequent transformations,

00:20:01.100 --> 00:20:03.670
like you filter out all the
successful trips so that you're

00:20:03.670 --> 00:20:05.140
only left with that.

00:20:05.140 --> 00:20:09.710
You create a data structure
with a count of one per event.

00:20:09.710 --> 00:20:11.330
And then you just do a group by.

00:20:11.330 --> 00:20:14.220
And then you're left with the
count for all of the drivers.

00:20:14.220 --> 00:20:15.890
So this is a very
basic example of how

00:20:15.890 --> 00:20:18.680
you could define a feature,
although in our case oftentimes

00:20:18.680 --> 00:20:21.190
we use Java to define this.

00:20:21.190 --> 00:20:23.540
But it has a lot
more boilerplate.

00:20:23.540 --> 00:20:26.480
So now you've solved the feature
creation, and standardization,

00:20:26.480 --> 00:20:27.920
and consistency problem.

00:20:27.920 --> 00:20:30.690
But now we need to
introduce storage as well.

00:20:30.690 --> 00:20:35.660
So for training we
introduced BigQuery.

00:20:35.660 --> 00:20:38.450
So BigQuery is just a
no-brainer store for us.

00:20:38.450 --> 00:20:41.120
We looked at a lot of
competing databases

00:20:41.120 --> 00:20:42.860
to store training
data, but ultimately

00:20:42.860 --> 00:20:44.180
it came down to one thing--

00:20:44.180 --> 00:20:48.110
our label data is generally in
the data sources as raw data.

00:20:48.110 --> 00:20:51.920
And our feature data often needs
to be joined onto our label

00:20:51.920 --> 00:20:54.320
data to create training sets.

00:20:54.320 --> 00:20:57.200
So we didn't want to have a
completely distinct training

00:20:57.200 --> 00:21:02.100
store, or at least not if we
didn't have a good reason.

00:21:02.100 --> 00:21:05.622
So we opted for BigQuery
because of its high scalability

00:21:05.622 --> 00:21:07.580
and the fact that it's
a completely cloud-based

00:21:07.580 --> 00:21:09.830
service-- you don't have to
manage any infrastructure.

00:21:09.830 --> 00:21:12.890
Easy to access, SQL-based.

00:21:12.890 --> 00:21:14.480
For this use case
it's also very good,

00:21:14.480 --> 00:21:17.960
because generally
features are represented

00:21:17.960 --> 00:21:20.560
as columns in this data store.

00:21:20.560 --> 00:21:22.100
And BigQuery is
a columnar store.

00:21:22.100 --> 00:21:25.830
So it's very efficient
at querying feature data.

00:21:25.830 --> 00:21:28.520
So that's what we introduced
for our training store.

00:21:28.520 --> 00:21:32.480
It's also very closely
integrated with other cloud

00:21:32.480 --> 00:21:34.400
services on Google Cloud.

00:21:34.400 --> 00:21:36.740
But we'll get to that later.

00:21:36.740 --> 00:21:39.350
So for serving we
introduced two data stores.

00:21:39.350 --> 00:21:40.970
The first is Bigtable
and the second

00:21:40.970 --> 00:21:44.810
is Cloud Memorystore, or
Google's hosted Redis.

00:21:44.810 --> 00:21:46.700
Bigtable was really a
game-changer for us,

00:21:46.700 --> 00:21:49.634
not just for features but for
other applications as well.

00:21:49.634 --> 00:21:51.050
And the reason why
it's so good is

00:21:51.050 --> 00:21:54.900
because it allows you to
consistently access feature

00:21:54.900 --> 00:21:56.480
data at very low latency--

00:21:56.480 --> 00:21:59.810
so, less than 10
milliseconds guaranteed.

00:21:59.810 --> 00:22:01.590
It allows you to handle
a very high load.

00:22:01.590 --> 00:22:04.160
So you can write and read
to it up to 10,000 times

00:22:04.160 --> 00:22:07.250
per second combined per node.

00:22:07.250 --> 00:22:10.320
And if you want to scale
up, you just add more nodes.

00:22:10.320 --> 00:22:13.610
So you can scale up linearly
to any amount of nodes

00:22:13.610 --> 00:22:15.800
with Bigtable.

00:22:15.800 --> 00:22:19.370
And then we also introduced
Cloud Memorystore or Redis

00:22:19.370 --> 00:22:20.960
for a similar use case.

00:22:20.960 --> 00:22:23.390
So sometimes when you
have hundreds of thousands

00:22:23.390 --> 00:22:25.826
of drivers there's
so many features,

00:22:25.826 --> 00:22:27.200
and some of those
features change

00:22:27.200 --> 00:22:30.290
so frequently, that the
load is just incredible.

00:22:30.290 --> 00:22:34.370
So you're going to have to
spin up maybe 50 Bigtable nodes

00:22:34.370 --> 00:22:35.550
to handle that load.

00:22:35.550 --> 00:22:38.510
So let's say you need
to update some features

00:22:38.510 --> 00:22:42.780
at a rate of 200,000 or
300,000 times per second.

00:22:42.780 --> 00:22:46.579
It doesn't make sense to use
Bigtable for that use case.

00:22:46.579 --> 00:22:48.120
So we introduced
Redis, because Redis

00:22:48.120 --> 00:22:50.975
allows you to read and write
at an extremely high rate.

00:22:50.975 --> 00:22:52.350
Often for these
types of features

00:22:52.350 --> 00:22:55.150
we don't care if the
data store goes down.

00:22:55.150 --> 00:22:57.930
So, the durability of Redis
is not really a concern for.

00:22:57.930 --> 00:22:58.640
Us

00:22:58.640 --> 00:23:01.540
And then ultimately we slaped
on top of this whole system

00:23:01.540 --> 00:23:02.760
a feature serving API.

00:23:02.760 --> 00:23:05.130
This API just
intelligently finds

00:23:05.130 --> 00:23:06.510
where the features are located.

00:23:06.510 --> 00:23:08.430
So if a feature look-up
request comes in,

00:23:08.430 --> 00:23:10.500
it breaks apart that
request, and finds the data,

00:23:10.500 --> 00:23:11.880
and joins it back.

00:23:11.880 --> 00:23:14.400
And then also very
importantly, there's

00:23:14.400 --> 00:23:16.710
also caching in the
feature serving API.

00:23:16.710 --> 00:23:19.500
Without that caching,
it's extremely difficult

00:23:19.500 --> 00:23:22.240
to handle the load.

00:23:22.240 --> 00:23:24.014
But now if you
look at this chart,

00:23:24.014 --> 00:23:26.430
you actually have a lot of
information about the features.

00:23:26.430 --> 00:23:28.430
You've got Dataflow where
you're creating your features.

00:23:28.430 --> 00:23:30.240
You know what features
you've defined.

00:23:30.240 --> 00:23:31.740
You've got the
access on the feature

00:23:31.740 --> 00:23:33.120
serving API-- like
which features

00:23:33.120 --> 00:23:34.470
are being used the most.

00:23:34.470 --> 00:23:40.340
And then you've got BigQuery
where you're creating training

00:23:40.340 --> 00:23:41.784
data sets and training models.

00:23:41.784 --> 00:23:43.200
And you know how
good those models

00:23:43.200 --> 00:23:45.270
are performing at inference.

00:23:45.270 --> 00:23:47.280
So with all this
data, if you log it,

00:23:47.280 --> 00:23:49.280
it's very useful to data
scientists, actually.

00:23:49.280 --> 00:23:51.810
And so what we did
is we just dumped

00:23:51.810 --> 00:23:54.960
all of that metadata about
features into a database.

00:23:54.960 --> 00:23:57.750
And then we just slapped
on top of that Data Studio.

00:23:57.750 --> 00:23:59.580
So this is just a
BI tool essentially

00:23:59.580 --> 00:24:01.620
that allows you to look at data.

00:24:01.620 --> 00:24:05.460
And we can see the
relative efficacy or impact

00:24:05.460 --> 00:24:08.520
that specific features
have relative to each other

00:24:08.520 --> 00:24:11.250
for predicting
outcomes or optimizing

00:24:11.250 --> 00:24:12.531
for specific objectives.

00:24:12.531 --> 00:24:14.280
And if you expose this
to data scientists,

00:24:14.280 --> 00:24:18.840
then it's a really powerful
tool for some key insights.

00:24:18.840 --> 00:24:21.270
And also, even if you didn't
have any of the impact,

00:24:21.270 --> 00:24:22.729
if you just had a
list of features,

00:24:22.729 --> 00:24:24.436
that's already useful
to data scientists.

00:24:24.436 --> 00:24:26.010
Because then they
can take this list

00:24:26.010 --> 00:24:27.718
and say, OK, I'm going
to go to BigQuery,

00:24:27.718 --> 00:24:32.500
and just select these specific
features, and train my model.

00:24:32.500 --> 00:24:34.530
And then finally, this
is what we've built.

00:24:34.530 --> 00:24:35.970
So we've got our data sources.

00:24:35.970 --> 00:24:37.890
We've got a standard
way in which

00:24:37.890 --> 00:24:40.937
Darius can define a feature to
transform those data sources.

00:24:40.937 --> 00:24:43.020
He doesn't have to worry
about the training store.

00:24:43.020 --> 00:24:45.832
He doesn't have to worry
about Memorystore or Bigtable.

00:24:45.832 --> 00:24:47.790
He doesn't have to worry
about the serving API.

00:24:47.790 --> 00:24:50.580
All he needs to do is find
features in the explorer,

00:24:50.580 --> 00:24:52.770
select them in BigQuery,
and train his model,

00:24:52.770 --> 00:24:54.570
and then remember
which features are

00:24:54.570 --> 00:24:56.970
available in the
feature serving API.

00:24:59.550 --> 00:25:03.250
So we've solved his
driver allocation problem

00:25:03.250 --> 00:25:05.530
to a large degree because
the feature engineering

00:25:05.530 --> 00:25:06.880
was the toughest part for him.

00:25:06.880 --> 00:25:12.010
All he needs to do now is define
features as feature transforms.

00:25:12.010 --> 00:25:15.060
The final part that we're going
to look at is machine learning.

00:25:15.060 --> 00:25:16.990
So we've got both of
the foundational blocks

00:25:16.990 --> 00:25:19.740
now of the data sources
and feature engineering.

00:25:19.740 --> 00:25:21.680
So let's have a look
at some of the projects

00:25:21.680 --> 00:25:23.880
that Darius was looking
at or was working on

00:25:23.880 --> 00:25:26.860
for machine learning.

00:25:26.860 --> 00:25:28.900
So dynamic pricing is
one of the key things

00:25:28.900 --> 00:25:33.610
that all ride-hailing companies
must make or must build.

00:25:33.610 --> 00:25:35.892
So the problem
here is that if you

00:25:35.892 --> 00:25:37.600
don't have dynamic
pricing, then you have

00:25:37.600 --> 00:25:39.900
inefficiencies in your market.

00:25:39.900 --> 00:25:41.830
So you have customers
and drivers,

00:25:41.830 --> 00:25:43.630
and they need to be matched.

00:25:43.630 --> 00:25:46.480
The drivers need to
service your customers.

00:25:46.480 --> 00:25:48.580
But if there's not enough
drivers in an area,

00:25:48.580 --> 00:25:50.829
you need to incentivize
drivers to move into that area

00:25:50.829 --> 00:25:52.360
and service those customers.

00:25:52.360 --> 00:25:55.390
And you do this by
having variable pricing

00:25:55.390 --> 00:25:57.320
in specific regions.

00:25:57.320 --> 00:26:00.580
So here's just an example
of a need map in Jakarta

00:26:00.580 --> 00:26:03.280
where the center of
Jakarta has higher prices

00:26:03.280 --> 00:26:05.080
than in the outskirts.

00:26:05.080 --> 00:26:08.050
And this is just to incentivize
drivers to move into that area

00:26:08.050 --> 00:26:10.250
to service those customers.

00:26:10.250 --> 00:26:14.080
So when we started with
this model or this project,

00:26:14.080 --> 00:26:16.480
Darius said, OK, he's
just going to take

00:26:16.480 --> 00:26:18.940
data that's on BigQuery,
train an ML model,

00:26:18.940 --> 00:26:20.480
and he's going to deploy it.

00:26:20.480 --> 00:26:22.605
And that's what he did--
he trained a model on some

00:26:22.605 --> 00:26:24.820
of the data, he had some
ideas, and he deployed it

00:26:24.820 --> 00:26:29.860
on the outskirts of one of the
towns as a small experiment.

00:26:29.860 --> 00:26:31.880
But there were some problems.

00:26:31.880 --> 00:26:33.922
The first problem he
had was it didn't scale.

00:26:33.922 --> 00:26:35.380
So his model was
written in Python.

00:26:35.380 --> 00:26:38.450
It couldn't scale out of
that experimental base.

00:26:38.450 --> 00:26:40.360
The second problem
was with this model

00:26:40.360 --> 00:26:42.670
that it was not interpretable.

00:26:42.670 --> 00:26:43.990
It was a black box.

00:26:43.990 --> 00:26:45.790
And nobody else in
the organization

00:26:45.790 --> 00:26:47.440
could explain what it was doing.

00:26:47.440 --> 00:26:48.940
And the third problem
was even if we

00:26:48.940 --> 00:26:51.050
could look at the
results of the model,

00:26:51.050 --> 00:26:53.140
we didn't know if
it was good or bad.

00:26:53.140 --> 00:26:55.960
Because this is actually an
extremely challenging problem

00:26:55.960 --> 00:26:56.987
to solve.

00:26:56.987 --> 00:26:58.570
It's very difficult
to have a baseline

00:26:58.570 --> 00:27:02.447
or a control in this
dynamic pricing problem.

00:27:02.447 --> 00:27:04.030
If you have an
experiment where you're

00:27:04.030 --> 00:27:05.800
raising prices in
one area, you're

00:27:05.800 --> 00:27:08.180
affecting essentially
the whole world.

00:27:08.180 --> 00:27:11.512
There's no isolation or
apples to apples comparison.

00:27:11.512 --> 00:27:12.970
So if you're raising
prices, you're

00:27:12.970 --> 00:27:15.130
pulling in supply
from external areas.

00:27:15.130 --> 00:27:16.880
And we had other issues as well.

00:27:16.880 --> 00:27:20.180
So let's say you've got food
as one of your products-- food

00:27:20.180 --> 00:27:20.680
delivery.

00:27:20.680 --> 00:27:22.450
And then you've
also got essentially

00:27:22.450 --> 00:27:25.060
people delivery on Go-Ride.

00:27:25.060 --> 00:27:27.290
They share a supply base.

00:27:27.290 --> 00:27:30.010
So if you raise the price
on the Go-Ride side,

00:27:30.010 --> 00:27:32.890
then you're taking riders or
people that deliver food away

00:27:32.890 --> 00:27:35.050
from your other product.

00:27:35.050 --> 00:27:37.000
And this is what
we found, that we

00:27:37.000 --> 00:27:40.540
needed to have a very clear
balance between our products

00:27:40.540 --> 00:27:43.210
in terms of dynamic pricing.

00:27:43.210 --> 00:27:46.270
So this was our realization-- do
machine learning like the great

00:27:46.270 --> 00:27:48.910
engineer you are, not like the
great machine learning expert

00:27:48.910 --> 00:27:50.710
you aren't.

00:27:50.710 --> 00:27:51.960
And this was important for us.

00:27:51.960 --> 00:27:54.790
So the reason for
this is that we

00:27:54.790 --> 00:27:57.460
knew we were making a
mistake by jumping into ML.

00:27:57.460 --> 00:28:00.920
So what we did first is
we defined our objective.

00:28:00.920 --> 00:28:05.260
We built a very clear, very
solid engineering system

00:28:05.260 --> 00:28:07.660
that could handle
the load and that

00:28:07.660 --> 00:28:10.930
could handle a very basic
mathematical model--

00:28:10.930 --> 00:28:12.830
not a machine learning model.

00:28:12.830 --> 00:28:16.420
And we deployed this
with clear objectives;

00:28:16.420 --> 00:28:18.311
with a clear
measurement of success;

00:28:18.311 --> 00:28:20.560
with clear dashboards, and
monitoring, and everything;

00:28:20.560 --> 00:28:23.830
without any machine learning.

00:28:23.830 --> 00:28:25.810
And then we had dynamic pricing.

00:28:25.810 --> 00:28:28.420
And now drivers could
see in specific areas

00:28:28.420 --> 00:28:30.670
where the prices
were higher or lower.

00:28:30.670 --> 00:28:32.470
And then they could
service those areas.

00:28:32.470 --> 00:28:34.150
They were incentivized
to go there.

00:28:34.150 --> 00:28:35.860
Ultimately, we
would end up adding

00:28:35.860 --> 00:28:37.720
machine learning to the system.

00:28:37.720 --> 00:28:40.690
But at the start,
the key thing was

00:28:40.690 --> 00:28:43.600
knowing what your
success criteria is

00:28:43.600 --> 00:28:47.560
and building a rock solid
engineering system first.

00:28:47.560 --> 00:28:50.200
But there's still some
inefficiencies in the system.

00:28:50.200 --> 00:28:52.930
So what a lot of the
drivers were complaining

00:28:52.930 --> 00:28:55.780
to Darius about was that, OK,
you can see that there are

00:28:55.780 --> 00:28:57.460
some areas that
are high in demand,

00:28:57.460 --> 00:29:00.410
but it's high in
demand for a reason,

00:29:00.410 --> 00:29:03.730
it's not high in
demand for no reason.

00:29:03.730 --> 00:29:05.920
The fact of the matter is
that there's high traffic,

00:29:05.920 --> 00:29:07.878
or there's a bottleneck,
or there's some reason

00:29:07.878 --> 00:29:09.920
why it's hard to get there.

00:29:09.920 --> 00:29:13.330
So if they had some way to
know in advance that there's

00:29:13.330 --> 00:29:15.550
going to be high demand,
that would help them.

00:29:15.550 --> 00:29:17.416
And this is where
forecasting came in.

00:29:17.416 --> 00:29:18.790
Darius new that
he could actually

00:29:18.790 --> 00:29:22.300
forecast what the demand
would be like in those areas.

00:29:22.300 --> 00:29:23.585
And so he set about--

00:29:23.585 --> 00:29:25.270
or this is what he told us--

00:29:25.270 --> 00:29:27.790
building a model that
would do forecasting.

00:29:27.790 --> 00:29:30.470
So he would take
all of the data--

00:29:30.470 --> 00:29:33.550
all of the supply data,
demand data, traffic data,

00:29:33.550 --> 00:29:35.920
user clickstream
data, all the signals

00:29:35.920 --> 00:29:38.440
that he could get on
a per-region basis.

00:29:38.440 --> 00:29:40.565
So this is an extremely
large amount of data--

00:29:40.565 --> 00:29:42.940
terabytes and terabytes of
data that he was streaming in.

00:29:45.670 --> 00:29:47.560
This model was so
complex that we even

00:29:47.560 --> 00:29:50.500
took both weather
stations in Jakarta

00:29:50.500 --> 00:29:52.630
and streamed in weather data.

00:29:52.630 --> 00:29:54.730
Because if rain
falls in Jakarta,

00:29:54.730 --> 00:29:58.000
then traffic will immediately
change very quickly.

00:29:58.000 --> 00:29:59.500
So what he wanted
to do is he wanted

00:29:59.500 --> 00:30:01.540
to take all these data
through a TF model

00:30:01.540 --> 00:30:04.720
and then predict on a per-region
basis or a per-area basis

00:30:04.720 --> 00:30:06.820
what the demand would be.

00:30:06.820 --> 00:30:10.030
But he ran into some
challenges as usual.

00:30:10.030 --> 00:30:13.420
So one of the
problems he ran into

00:30:13.420 --> 00:30:15.520
was that he couldn't
actually train

00:30:15.520 --> 00:30:17.980
this model on his local
machine, even as just

00:30:17.980 --> 00:30:20.080
a test on a subset of the data.

00:30:20.080 --> 00:30:23.620
And when he tried to move the
data onto a virtual machine

00:30:23.620 --> 00:30:25.649
and train it there,
it also fell over.

00:30:25.649 --> 00:30:27.940
The virtual machine just
couldn't load all of this data

00:30:27.940 --> 00:30:29.090
into memory.

00:30:29.090 --> 00:30:31.620
So what he did is he asked
a bunch of engineers,

00:30:31.620 --> 00:30:34.240
can you help me spin
up a Spark cluster

00:30:34.240 --> 00:30:35.960
and train the model there.

00:30:35.960 --> 00:30:37.660
So they did that and it worked.

00:30:37.660 --> 00:30:39.820
They used Spark, and
they trained the model,

00:30:39.820 --> 00:30:42.370
and they could
actually deploy it.

00:30:42.370 --> 00:30:44.290
But now there's a
problem because Darius is

00:30:44.290 --> 00:30:45.610
dependent on these engineers.

00:30:45.610 --> 00:30:46.880
Every time he wants
to make a change,

00:30:46.880 --> 00:30:48.490
he needs to ask the
engineers, can you

00:30:48.490 --> 00:30:50.280
help me with this cluster.

00:30:50.280 --> 00:30:52.990
And another problem he had
was that it was actually

00:30:52.990 --> 00:30:53.814
quite slow.

00:30:53.814 --> 00:30:55.480
It would take hours--
and sometimes more

00:30:55.480 --> 00:30:58.330
than a day-- to
train this model.

00:30:58.330 --> 00:31:02.700
So the lesson here was
don't break abstraction--

00:31:02.700 --> 00:31:05.252
if you're a data
scientist, try and operate

00:31:05.252 --> 00:31:07.460
with the appropriate tooling
at the abstraction layer

00:31:07.460 --> 00:31:10.490
that you're comfortable with
and that you're an expert at.

00:31:10.490 --> 00:31:12.920
Don't drop down into
the engineering world

00:31:12.920 --> 00:31:15.570
unless you absolutely have to.

00:31:15.570 --> 00:31:18.020
And as a data science
platform lead,

00:31:18.020 --> 00:31:20.060
it's my responsibility
to give Darius

00:31:20.060 --> 00:31:23.810
the tools that he needs to
operate at this abstraction

00:31:23.810 --> 00:31:24.500
layer.

00:31:24.500 --> 00:31:28.530
So what we introduced to
Darius was Cloud ML Engine.

00:31:28.530 --> 00:31:30.110
So this is one of
the reasons we also

00:31:30.110 --> 00:31:37.550
chose BigQuery as our feature
and world data warehouse.

00:31:37.550 --> 00:31:40.010
So it's got a very
close integration

00:31:40.010 --> 00:31:41.336
with Cloud ML Engine.

00:31:41.336 --> 00:31:42.710
So with Cloud ML
Engine it allows

00:31:42.710 --> 00:31:45.410
you to train TensorFlow models.

00:31:45.410 --> 00:31:47.090
It's a completely
managed service.

00:31:47.090 --> 00:31:48.200
It scales automatically.

00:31:48.200 --> 00:31:49.435
Training is distributed.

00:31:49.435 --> 00:31:50.810
And there's some
other advantages

00:31:50.810 --> 00:31:53.390
like you've got hyper
parameter tuning, which

00:31:53.390 --> 00:31:55.015
means your models
can be more accurate.

00:31:55.015 --> 00:31:56.973
You've got levers that
you can pull if you want

00:31:56.973 --> 00:31:58.210
to iterate and train faster.

00:31:58.210 --> 00:32:01.480
You can go from
CPUs, GPus, TPUs.

00:32:01.480 --> 00:32:03.350
So this is what we
introduced to Darius.

00:32:03.350 --> 00:32:05.790
And the way he used
it was as follows.

00:32:05.790 --> 00:32:07.700
He's got his feature
transformations already.

00:32:07.700 --> 00:32:10.250
So he's got his Dataflow
creating features

00:32:10.250 --> 00:32:11.210
into BigQuery.

00:32:11.210 --> 00:32:12.590
Those are the time
series signals

00:32:12.590 --> 00:32:14.030
that he is getting
from the field,

00:32:14.030 --> 00:32:16.940
like the weather
data that I spoke of.

00:32:16.940 --> 00:32:18.440
He trains a model
on Cloud ML Engine

00:32:18.440 --> 00:32:20.720
and he serves it
on Cloud ML Engine.

00:32:20.720 --> 00:32:25.560
Then his signals are
coming from Kafka as well.

00:32:25.560 --> 00:32:27.140
So these are the raw events.

00:32:27.140 --> 00:32:29.960
And then he's got a separate
stream that does inference.

00:32:29.960 --> 00:32:31.460
So this is just a
Cloud Dataflow job

00:32:31.460 --> 00:32:34.310
that triggers serving
on Cloud ML Engine.

00:32:34.310 --> 00:32:37.070
So if rain falls, it will
trigger Cloud ML Engine

00:32:37.070 --> 00:32:39.170
for new inference,
predict the demand,

00:32:39.170 --> 00:32:42.510
and then publish
it back to Kafka.

00:32:42.510 --> 00:32:44.230
And so this worked.

00:32:44.230 --> 00:32:46.170
One of the downsides to
using Cloud ML Engine

00:32:46.170 --> 00:32:48.145
is that it only supports
training for TensorFlow.

00:32:48.145 --> 00:32:50.394
So if you want to use XGBoost,
or scikit-learn, or any

00:32:50.394 --> 00:32:52.350
of these, it doesn't
work for training.

00:32:52.350 --> 00:32:54.960
You can serve those
other types of models.

00:32:54.960 --> 00:32:56.350
But there are some downsides.

00:32:56.350 --> 00:32:58.380
But for us, the key
thing was we wanted

00:32:58.380 --> 00:33:00.930
to have at least one tool
that the data scientists could

00:33:00.930 --> 00:33:03.630
use without having
to pull in engineers.

00:33:06.240 --> 00:33:07.590
So then we had forecasting.

00:33:07.590 --> 00:33:10.150
So these are just three
of the different regions.

00:33:10.150 --> 00:33:11.662
So here's a 30-minute forecast.

00:33:11.662 --> 00:33:13.620
The green light represents
one of the forecasts

00:33:13.620 --> 00:33:17.300
and the yellow line
represents the actual values.

00:33:17.300 --> 00:33:19.630
And so we knew what the
demand would be in the future.

00:33:19.630 --> 00:33:21.870
We could tell drivers,
this is an area

00:33:21.870 --> 00:33:23.040
that will be high in demand.

00:33:23.040 --> 00:33:24.630
Or we could just
change the pricing,

00:33:24.630 --> 00:33:27.300
and that will also influence
the way the drivers react

00:33:27.300 --> 00:33:29.490
to the dynamics.

00:33:29.490 --> 00:33:31.810
But this wasn't Darius'
toughest challenge.

00:33:31.810 --> 00:33:33.360
Darius had other
challenges as well.

00:33:37.746 --> 00:33:39.120
The toughest
challenge Darius had

00:33:39.120 --> 00:33:41.320
was on the home screen
of our application.

00:33:41.320 --> 00:33:43.110
So this is the home screen.

00:33:43.110 --> 00:33:45.580
And you can see at the top
there are some products--

00:33:45.580 --> 00:33:47.230
like Go-Ride, and
Go-Car, and those.

00:33:47.230 --> 00:33:49.710
But at the bottom, you
can actually scroll down.

00:33:49.710 --> 00:33:51.570
There's a very long feed.

00:33:51.570 --> 00:33:54.930
And we have full control over
what we display on that feed.

00:33:54.930 --> 00:33:56.700
And for us, it's
extremely important

00:33:56.700 --> 00:33:58.410
to personalize our application.

00:33:58.410 --> 00:34:00.600
All of our customers
use our application

00:34:00.600 --> 00:34:01.690
for different reasons.

00:34:01.690 --> 00:34:03.360
Some order food,
some buy groceries,

00:34:03.360 --> 00:34:05.560
some use it as a
ride-hailing application.

00:34:05.560 --> 00:34:08.190
If you have 18 plus products,
you need to customize it.

00:34:08.190 --> 00:34:11.010
And as a business,
you have objectives

00:34:11.010 --> 00:34:13.840
like you want to introduce new
products to certain customers.

00:34:13.840 --> 00:34:17.340
So this is a very important
part of our application.

00:34:17.340 --> 00:34:20.290
So Darius was tasked
with building this model.

00:34:20.290 --> 00:34:24.760
What his model would get
is the user information--

00:34:24.760 --> 00:34:28.590
so, the user's ID,
the user's location,

00:34:28.590 --> 00:34:31.770
and the time at which the user
has opened the application.

00:34:31.770 --> 00:34:33.270
And then what he
has to respond with

00:34:33.270 --> 00:34:38.790
is a list of recommendations to
personalize this home screen.

00:34:38.790 --> 00:34:40.560
But this is actually
quite challenging.

00:34:40.560 --> 00:34:42.960
Because that data is
only available to us

00:34:42.960 --> 00:34:44.909
when the user opens
the application.

00:34:44.909 --> 00:34:47.850
So the trick here is that we
don't track users when they're

00:34:47.850 --> 00:34:49.638
not inside of our application.

00:34:49.638 --> 00:34:51.179
So we don't know
where or when you're

00:34:51.179 --> 00:34:53.790
going to open the
application until you do it.

00:34:53.790 --> 00:34:57.780
And the type of personalization
we want Darius described it as

00:34:57.780 --> 00:34:59.230
follows.

00:34:59.230 --> 00:35:02.820
He said that let's say you
know that a person will

00:35:02.820 --> 00:35:06.120
order a pizza for his
children on a Friday night.

00:35:06.120 --> 00:35:08.970
He's been doing it for
three weeks in a row.

00:35:08.970 --> 00:35:12.630
So you want to be able to
recommend buying a pizza to him

00:35:12.630 --> 00:35:15.862
if he arrives at home on
that Friday night again.

00:35:15.862 --> 00:35:17.570
But if he arrives at
the office, of if he

00:35:17.570 --> 00:35:19.350
arrives at a friend's
house, then you

00:35:19.350 --> 00:35:21.130
don't want to recommend that.

00:35:21.130 --> 00:35:25.900
So that's the type of control
we want in personalization.

00:35:25.900 --> 00:35:28.500
So the challenge is,
of course, that we

00:35:28.500 --> 00:35:31.829
don't know where or when
the user will pop up.

00:35:31.829 --> 00:35:33.620
So Darius said, OK,
fine, he's going to try

00:35:33.620 --> 00:35:36.810
and the model to see
if he can serve this.

00:35:39.450 --> 00:35:41.400
So here are his
targets on the right.

00:35:41.400 --> 00:35:44.030
So he needs to serve a
response in 30 milliseconds.

00:35:44.030 --> 00:35:45.900
It's the home screen
of the application,

00:35:45.900 --> 00:35:48.170
so you have to serve
responses very quickly.

00:35:48.170 --> 00:35:51.710
Our SLO is at 10,000
requests per second.

00:35:51.710 --> 00:35:53.780
So the throughput is very high.

00:35:53.780 --> 00:35:55.340
And with international
expansion,

00:35:55.340 --> 00:35:58.460
we knew we needed to support
even higher throughput soon.

00:35:58.460 --> 00:36:01.850
So this model that he
built was an application.

00:36:01.850 --> 00:36:03.680
It was embedded
within an application.

00:36:03.680 --> 00:36:06.240
And it was before we had
the feature platform.

00:36:06.240 --> 00:36:08.600
So in this case, he
actually had user data

00:36:08.600 --> 00:36:11.600
that was loaded into the
application statically

00:36:11.600 --> 00:36:13.070
as batch data.

00:36:13.070 --> 00:36:16.460
So he would get the user ID,
the time, and the Lat/Long

00:36:16.460 --> 00:36:18.590
when the user opens
the application.

00:36:18.590 --> 00:36:21.440
And then what he would
do is he would transform

00:36:21.440 --> 00:36:24.890
based on the user data as
well as the incoming request

00:36:24.890 --> 00:36:27.560
new features-- he would
generate new features--

00:36:27.560 --> 00:36:30.200
put it into the TensorFlow
model, do inference.

00:36:30.200 --> 00:36:32.210
And then it'll produce a
list of recommendations

00:36:32.210 --> 00:36:34.810
which he would return.

00:36:34.810 --> 00:36:37.560
And so what Darius is
he did a benchmark.

00:36:37.560 --> 00:36:39.810
He just had a look
at what performance

00:36:39.810 --> 00:36:43.290
he's actually getting
with this application.

00:36:43.290 --> 00:36:45.540
And this was his
initial performance.

00:36:45.540 --> 00:36:48.290
His target is 30 milliseconds,
but he was serving results

00:36:48.290 --> 00:36:51.900
at 140 milliseconds, which is
almost five times too slow.

00:36:51.900 --> 00:36:54.930
So, as any engineer
would tell you,

00:36:54.930 --> 00:36:58.060
the first thing you do in
Kubernetes is you scale it out.

00:36:58.060 --> 00:36:59.740
And that's what we did.

00:36:59.740 --> 00:37:02.167
Of course that doesn't
really solve your problem.

00:37:02.167 --> 00:37:03.750
There's a certain
amount of complexity

00:37:03.750 --> 00:37:07.680
in producing these results
and doing inference.

00:37:07.680 --> 00:37:09.610
And scaling up will
just reduce the load.

00:37:09.610 --> 00:37:13.352
It won't actually minimize
the latency that much.

00:37:13.352 --> 00:37:15.810
So then eventually what we did
is we introduced the feature

00:37:15.810 --> 00:37:17.470
platform.

00:37:17.470 --> 00:37:19.560
And by doing that,
we could externalize

00:37:19.560 --> 00:37:22.320
a lot of the features
outside of this application

00:37:22.320 --> 00:37:26.670
so that only the model is
hosted in the application.

00:37:26.670 --> 00:37:28.030
And so that's what we did.

00:37:28.030 --> 00:37:30.090
But the latency
was still too slow.

00:37:30.090 --> 00:37:32.190
It was taking too
long to serve results.

00:37:32.190 --> 00:37:35.850
And we couldn't actually deploy
this at scale in production.

00:37:35.850 --> 00:37:38.800
It would be too disruptive
to the user experience.

00:37:38.800 --> 00:37:40.102
And we really hit a wall here.

00:37:40.102 --> 00:37:41.310
And this is a common problem.

00:37:41.310 --> 00:37:45.570
Because each project has a
unique model with its own

00:37:45.570 --> 00:37:49.470
characteristics, a
completely unique system.

00:37:49.470 --> 00:37:53.580
And so we mulled over this
and we came to a realization--

00:37:53.580 --> 00:37:55.650
we should pre-compute
everything.

00:37:55.650 --> 00:37:58.740
Of course, in this case
it's not that easy to just

00:37:58.740 --> 00:38:00.270
pre-compute everything.

00:38:00.270 --> 00:38:02.302
Because you don't know
where users will be,

00:38:02.302 --> 00:38:04.510
and you don't know when
they'll open the application,

00:38:04.510 --> 00:38:06.150
and you don't know
which user it will be.

00:38:06.150 --> 00:38:07.890
So the amount of
combinations is immense.

00:38:07.890 --> 00:38:11.420
If you have tens of
millions of users,

00:38:11.420 --> 00:38:13.710
you need to somehow
reduce the amount

00:38:13.710 --> 00:38:16.200
of predictions or
recommendations

00:38:16.200 --> 00:38:17.790
that you want to pre-compute.

00:38:17.790 --> 00:38:20.860
And the way we did that
is by doing two things.

00:38:20.860 --> 00:38:24.460
The first is we bucket
locations into areas.

00:38:24.460 --> 00:38:28.770
So we say all the locations
within a football field size

00:38:28.770 --> 00:38:30.300
area counts as that area.

00:38:30.300 --> 00:38:32.700
And then we bucket time
into windows of, let's say,

00:38:32.700 --> 00:38:34.245
30 minutes or an hour.

00:38:34.245 --> 00:38:36.040
Then for each customer--

00:38:36.040 --> 00:38:37.980
so, for each of our
millions of customers,

00:38:37.980 --> 00:38:39.962
and for each of these
tens of thousands

00:38:39.962 --> 00:38:42.420
or hundreds of thousands of
areas, and for each of his time

00:38:42.420 --> 00:38:47.940
buckets we produce a customized,
personalized recommendation.

00:38:47.940 --> 00:38:52.290
But you're still talking
about hundreds or more--

00:38:52.290 --> 00:38:55.600
billions-- of recommendations
for a single model.

00:38:55.600 --> 00:38:57.990
So this is really, really
big data sets, terabytes

00:38:57.990 --> 00:39:00.070
and terabytes of data.

00:39:00.070 --> 00:39:01.950
And of course the
solution that we presented

00:39:01.950 --> 00:39:04.600
was Bigtable to store this data.

00:39:04.600 --> 00:39:06.450
So it's one of the
few tools that we

00:39:06.450 --> 00:39:09.240
have found that can
actually store and serve

00:39:09.240 --> 00:39:11.800
all of these recommendations.

00:39:11.800 --> 00:39:16.050
So the way that we changed
Darius' system was as follows.

00:39:16.050 --> 00:39:19.800
So users now have specific
actions that they take.

00:39:19.800 --> 00:39:22.560
Let's say that you buy a
pizza on a Friday night.

00:39:22.560 --> 00:39:25.010
That is something that
describes your behavior.

00:39:25.010 --> 00:39:27.450
We logged that has
an event on Kafka.

00:39:27.450 --> 00:39:29.880
And then we have a
Dataflow job that hosts

00:39:29.880 --> 00:39:32.040
a model-- a TensorFlow model.

00:39:32.040 --> 00:39:34.920
And this model
streams in this event

00:39:34.920 --> 00:39:37.650
and then it produces
personalized recommendations

00:39:37.650 --> 00:39:41.920
for that user based on the
unique behavior of the user.

00:39:41.920 --> 00:39:44.010
And then it stores all
of these recommendations

00:39:44.010 --> 00:39:48.750
or it just updates the existing
recommendations in Bigtable.

00:39:48.750 --> 00:39:51.940
So this process continually
happens behind the scenes.

00:39:51.940 --> 00:39:54.000
And now our model
service has changed.

00:39:54.000 --> 00:39:56.762
Now our model service
is a lookup service.

00:39:56.762 --> 00:39:58.470
And so the lookup
service, all it's doing

00:39:58.470 --> 00:40:01.020
is it's just looking at
Bigtable at a specific landing

00:40:01.020 --> 00:40:03.160
zone where that
information is found.

00:40:03.160 --> 00:40:05.550
And it just retrieves
it and serves it.

00:40:05.550 --> 00:40:07.980
So if you think about
this, this paradigm shift

00:40:07.980 --> 00:40:09.600
is very powerful as an engineer.

00:40:09.600 --> 00:40:11.850
Because your machine
learning system

00:40:11.850 --> 00:40:15.930
is completely disconnected from
the serving infrastructure.

00:40:15.930 --> 00:40:17.910
If you delete or
if you remove all

00:40:17.910 --> 00:40:20.730
of the ML pipelines
and EventStream,

00:40:20.730 --> 00:40:23.640
you still have Bigtable
that's independently testable,

00:40:23.640 --> 00:40:25.680
that you know the
characteristics of.

00:40:25.680 --> 00:40:27.320
It can handle terabytes of data.

00:40:27.320 --> 00:40:30.130
It can serve responses
at 10 milliseconds.

00:40:30.130 --> 00:40:32.670
And you'll never
have an issue as long

00:40:32.670 --> 00:40:36.250
as you've tested at least
once for your use case.

00:40:36.250 --> 00:40:38.910
So this is what we
presented for Darius.

00:40:38.910 --> 00:40:41.420
And when we looked
at the performance,

00:40:41.420 --> 00:40:42.580
it was good enough--

00:40:42.580 --> 00:40:43.511
15 milliseconds.

00:40:43.511 --> 00:40:45.510
You're always going to
hit that 15 milliseconds.

00:40:45.510 --> 00:40:47.399
There's enough buffer.

00:40:47.399 --> 00:40:49.440
Of course this doesn't
solve all of your problems

00:40:49.440 --> 00:40:51.300
as an engineer or
a data scientist.

00:40:51.300 --> 00:40:53.670
Some models you
can't re-compute.

00:40:53.670 --> 00:40:56.830
Some you'll always have to
have a model in a service.

00:40:56.830 --> 00:40:58.650
But if you have
the option, think

00:40:58.650 --> 00:41:02.700
about storing very
large amounts of data

00:41:02.700 --> 00:41:05.610
as an option or an
alternative to serving models

00:41:05.610 --> 00:41:11.580
in production because of the
productionizational abilities

00:41:11.580 --> 00:41:13.440
of that.

00:41:13.440 --> 00:41:15.660
So what are the impacts
of these changes

00:41:15.660 --> 00:41:18.735
that we introduced to Darius?

00:41:18.735 --> 00:41:20.610
Originally when we looked
at Darius' workflow

00:41:20.610 --> 00:41:23.920
on a typical project, he
was spending a lot of time

00:41:23.920 --> 00:41:26.090
on provisioning infrastructure,
installing software,

00:41:26.090 --> 00:41:29.492
all of these tasks that were
not really data-science-related.

00:41:29.492 --> 00:41:30.950
We wanted him to
focus more on what

00:41:30.950 --> 00:41:33.440
he's good at, which
is creating features,

00:41:33.440 --> 00:41:37.140
developing models, evaluating
experiments, and things.

00:41:37.140 --> 00:41:40.790
So we introduced what is
essentially a platform built

00:41:40.790 --> 00:41:42.170
on Google Cloud.

00:41:42.170 --> 00:41:43.829
And if you look at
the data sources,

00:41:43.829 --> 00:41:45.870
we've got all the types
of data sources you want.

00:41:45.870 --> 00:41:47.270
Data [INAUDIBLE],,
you've got EventStream,

00:41:47.270 --> 00:41:49.222
you've got BigQuery as
your data warehouse.

00:41:49.222 --> 00:41:51.680
There's a single place where
Darius can define his features

00:41:51.680 --> 00:41:52.460
in Cloud Dataflow.

00:41:52.460 --> 00:41:55.280
He doesn't have to define
features anywhere else.

00:41:55.280 --> 00:41:57.380
He can do inference in
the stream on Dataflow

00:41:57.380 --> 00:42:00.410
by hosting Cloud ML
Models in Dataflow.

00:42:00.410 --> 00:42:03.530
Alternatively, he can also
host it on Cloud ML Engine.

00:42:03.530 --> 00:42:07.680
He's got a serving
store in Bigtable

00:42:07.680 --> 00:42:11.210
and in Cloud Memorystore, and
a training store in BigQuery.

00:42:11.210 --> 00:42:12.980
And he doesn't have
to manage those.

00:42:12.980 --> 00:42:15.920
Most of this infrastructure
is managed by us.

00:42:15.920 --> 00:42:18.380
And it's not really
managed by us as engineers,

00:42:18.380 --> 00:42:23.800
because it's all just cloud
services in most of the cases.

00:42:23.800 --> 00:42:26.870
So all Darius actually has to
worry about is doing modeling

00:42:26.870 --> 00:42:29.930
in Datalab, finding features
in the feature explorer,

00:42:29.930 --> 00:42:32.660
and then using BigQuery to
create his training sets,

00:42:32.660 --> 00:42:35.960
and using ML Engine
to train those models.

00:42:35.960 --> 00:42:39.320
Of course there will
always be custom projects.

00:42:39.320 --> 00:42:43.610
I think the key thing here is
that we introduce technologies

00:42:43.610 --> 00:42:46.510
that allows Darius to operate
at his abstraction layer

00:42:46.510 --> 00:42:48.590
where he doesn't need
to pull in engineers.

00:42:48.590 --> 00:42:50.240
He can do things on his own.

00:42:50.240 --> 00:42:51.830
And he can produce
results of his own.

00:42:51.830 --> 00:42:54.710
He doesn't have to
spin up infrastructure.

00:42:54.710 --> 00:42:59.480
So now Darius is using a lot
less time on a typical project.

00:42:59.480 --> 00:43:01.580
He's spending more
time developing models.

00:43:01.580 --> 00:43:04.490
And because the time has been
compressed, it's faster for him

00:43:04.490 --> 00:43:06.935
to get to market and he
can take on more projects.

00:43:09.630 --> 00:43:13.530
So the impact on Go-Jek is
our data scientists can now

00:43:13.530 --> 00:43:16.060
deliver projects faster.

00:43:16.060 --> 00:43:18.720
We have more touchpoints
within our application

00:43:18.720 --> 00:43:20.930
because we can
deliver more models.

00:43:20.930 --> 00:43:22.740
Our customer experience
has improved a lot

00:43:22.740 --> 00:43:25.170
because now the models are
more accurate because we

00:43:25.170 --> 00:43:27.810
have more features as well.

00:43:27.810 --> 00:43:30.030
We have less data
scientists per customer.

00:43:30.030 --> 00:43:32.860
And this is a great
thing, because expanding

00:43:32.860 --> 00:43:34.860
to new markets, you
don't necessarily

00:43:34.860 --> 00:43:37.200
want to just add
new data scientists.

00:43:37.200 --> 00:43:39.840
It is better to leverage
the existing ones you have.

00:43:39.840 --> 00:43:41.580
And obviously having
less infrastructure

00:43:41.580 --> 00:43:43.350
is a big one for us.

00:43:43.350 --> 00:43:46.210
It's less time to spend
managing infrastructure

00:43:46.210 --> 00:43:47.910
and it costs less.

00:43:47.910 --> 00:43:49.610
And just to recap the lessons.

00:43:49.610 --> 00:43:50.880
Let the data come to you.

00:43:50.880 --> 00:43:52.560
Centralize your data warehouse.

00:43:52.560 --> 00:43:55.150
Don't do anything else unless
you've done that first.

00:43:55.150 --> 00:43:58.470
Especially if you have a very
big geographically separated

00:43:58.470 --> 00:44:01.120
organization like
we do, for features

00:44:01.120 --> 00:44:03.271
you'll pay a cost
once, but then after

00:44:03.271 --> 00:44:04.770
that it should be
free and available

00:44:04.770 --> 00:44:08.130
in all of your environments,
and discoverable, and trackable.

00:44:08.130 --> 00:44:10.720
This is also a very
important step.

00:44:10.720 --> 00:44:13.120
And then the third one was
don't break abstraction.

00:44:13.120 --> 00:44:14.790
If you're a data
scientist, operate

00:44:14.790 --> 00:44:17.970
with the tools at
the abstraction layer

00:44:17.970 --> 00:44:21.060
that you're comfortable with,
that you're a domain expert at.

00:44:21.060 --> 00:44:23.130
And then finally, if
you have the option,

00:44:23.130 --> 00:44:24.600
pre-compute everything.

00:44:24.600 --> 00:44:27.721
Pre-compute results.

00:44:27.721 --> 00:44:28.220
OK.

00:44:28.220 --> 00:44:29.030
That's it for me.

00:44:29.030 --> 00:44:32.380
[MUSIC PLAYING]

